QR codes had a first life, around 2011, and it did not go well. They turned up on bus shelters and cereal boxes, and almost nobody scanned them, because scanning meant finding and installing a separate app first. The code was asking for a download before it would tell you what it was for.

That objection is gone. **Every current iPhone and Android phone reads a QR code from the camera app** — point, tap the link that appears, done. Nothing to install, nothing to sign up for. That one change is why codes that failed a decade ago work now.

So the question is no longer whether QR codes work. It is whether yours is in the right place, big enough, and saying something worth scanning.

## What makes a code get scanned

### Say what they get

This is the whole thing. A code on its own is a black square with no promise attached, and people do not scan squares out of curiosity.

Compare:

- **"Scan here"** — ignored.
- **"Scan for a free coffee on your tenth visit"** — scanned.
- **"Scan to join our loyalty card. No app, no sign-up."** — scanned, and it answers the objection before it is raised.

Put the words in a size people can read from where they are standing, and put them directly above or below the code, not on the other side of the poster.

### Make it big enough

The rule of thumb printers use is roughly **one part code to ten parts distance**: however far away the reader will be, the code wants to be about a tenth of that across.

| Where it goes | Scanned from | Code width |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Table card or receipt | 30cm | 3cm |
| By the till | 50cm | 5cm |
| A3 poster on a wall | 1.5m | 15cm |
| Shop window, read from the pavement | 2m | 20cm |

Err larger. A code that is slightly too big looks confident; one that is slightly too small simply fails, and the person does not try twice.

### Put it where someone is standing still

Scanning takes both hands and about four seconds of attention. That rules out most places.

Good spots:

- **By the till**, at the moment someone is waiting for a card machine anyway.
- **On the table**, if people sit down.
- **In the window at eye level**, where someone reading your opening hours is already standing.
- **On the receipt or the bag**, read later at home.

Bad spots: above head height, behind the counter, on a moving vehicle, on a screen that times out, or anywhere someone is walking past rather than standing.

### Test it before you print fifty

Print one and try it properly:

- **Through the window glass**, if that is where it is going, with the sun on it. Glare kills more codes than bad printing.
- **On a cheap old phone**, not just your own.
- **In your actual lighting**, which in most shops is dimmer than you think.
- **From the real distance**, standing where a customer would stand.

If it hesitates for more than a second or two, make it bigger.

## Send them to what you promised

The most common mistake is a code that goes to a homepage. Someone scanned because you offered a free coffee on their tenth visit; dropping them on a general website makes them hunt, and they will not.

Send the scan straight to the thing: the loyalty card sign-up, the specific offer, the booking page. One tap from the promise to the thing itself.

It is also worth sending scans through a link you control rather than printing a raw destination, so the code keeps working if the page behind it ever moves — and so you can count the scans.

{% cta heading="A QR poster for your window, made for you" text="Promotello generates a printable A4 poster with your loyalty card's code on it, sized and laid out for a shop window. Scans go through a link that counts them." target="features" /%}

## How do you know if it is working?

A QR code gives you something a paper flyer never could: a number. If your code goes through a link that counts scans, you can see exactly how many people used it, and when.

Watch three things:

1. **Scans per week.** Flat near zero usually means placement or wording, not the offer.
2. **What happens after the scan.** If people scan and then leave without joining or claiming, the page behind the code is the problem, not the poster.
3. **Where the scans come from.** Window versus till versus receipt will not be equal, and the winner is often not the one you expected.

If you have a window poster and a till card and one gets ten times the other, print more of the one that works and stop printing the other.

## The mistakes worth avoiding

- **A bare code with no words.** The code is the mechanism; the words are the reason.
- **Too small.** The commonest cause of a code that "does not work".
- **Linking to a homepage.** Make the scan land on the promise.
- **A code printed on a dark or busy photo.** Codes want quiet contrast — dark on light, with clear space around them.
- **Never testing it.** Print one, scan it from where a customer stands, then print the rest.
- **Leaving it up after the offer ends.** A dead code teaches people not to bother with your next one.

QR codes are not magic, and they are not a marketing strategy on their own. They are a way of removing the steps between "that sounds good" and actually doing it — which is also what [off-peak offers](/blog/how-to-fill-quiet-hours) and [loyalty cards](/blog/digital-vs-paper-loyalty-cards) are trying to do. The code is the shortest path between a poster and a phone, and that is worth getting right.
